// Practice quiz: Fixing CORS: Spring Security config vs Vite dev proxy
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/fetch-and-browser-client/fixing-cors-spring-security-config-vs-vite-dev-proxy

::o1 who enforces::Who enforces the same-origin rule that CORS relaxes? {
~The Spring Boot server #Not quite. The server only sends headers; the browser applies the rule.
~The operating system firewall #Not quite. A firewall plays no part in CORS.
~The JSON parser #Not quite. Parsing is unrelated.
=The browser, on behalf of the page #Correct. Tools such as curl and Postman do not enforce it.
}

::o1 origin::Which pair is cross-origin? {
=http\://localhost\:5173 and http\://localhost\:8080 #Correct. The ports differ, so the origins differ.
~http\://a.test/x and http\://a.test/y #Not quite. Paths are not part of the origin.
~https\://a.test and https\://a.test\:443 #Not quite. The default port for https is 443, so they match.
~http\://a.test/x?y\=1 and http\://a.test/x #Not quite. Queries are not part of the origin.
}

::o1 preflight trigger::Which request sends a preflight? {
~A plain GET with no custom headers #Not quite. That is a simple request.
=A POST with Content-Type application/json #Correct. That content type is outside the safelisted values.
~A POST with Content-Type text/plain #Not quite. text/plain is safelisted.
~A same-origin POST with JSON #Not quite. Same-origin requests do not use CORS.
}

::o2 proxy in production::Why is the Vite proxy not a production answer? {
=It exists only in the dev server, not in the built static files #Correct. A build outputs plain files, with no proxy.
~It is slower than CORS #Not quite. Speed is not the issue.
~It only works for GET #Not quite. It forwards any method.
~It requires Spring Security #Not quite. It has no dependence on Spring.
}

::o3 reading the evidence::Server logs show a plain GET handled with status 200, yet the browser console shows a CORS error. What happened? {
~The server never received the request #Not quite. The logs show it did.
~The JSON was invalid #Not quite. That would be a different error.
=The server answered, and the browser blocked your code from reading the response #Correct. The exchange succeeded; reading it did not.
~The status should have been 201 #Not quite. The status is not the cause.
}
